#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e6d12e2-e094-56bb-aad7-4875408f1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253794} {:answer/id #uuid "30cf8320-5fd5-5b11-82e3-deaed3de2914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12\\sqrt{3},-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254096} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5579c2-3d6f-5c86-bb18-f4835d982390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254508} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5c3da8-df30-5fe1-bff4-b1a94f639b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{3},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254767} {:answer/id #uuid "df0dcfd3-b874-5c42-adc8-6f258f9c28bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8\\sqrt{3},-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6d12e2-e094-56bb-aad7-4875408f1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253794} :answer-field/id #uuid "504a5f08-c553-5514-84d8-de8398b03057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cae813d1-464d-5870-965e-a7cef5a78bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-120719" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b1318b156281c7bc8724f988545d9f5c9fab2bda4ed88c387f051dbe5bece1e.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=12$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(12,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "742d9c7f-d4ee-56e4-91a5-257220e81235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254697} {:answer/id #uuid "77fef0cd-a14d-513e-a983-57f641d81912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254731} {:answer/id #uuid "be8416e5-45aa-50f0-ad1e-e88defc465b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255371} {:answer/id #uuid "cde2b163-41b2-5e57-b80d-41b421c63e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255499} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d5128f-097e-53b2-b6f7-370f46fe6294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be8416e5-45aa-50f0-ad1e-e88defc465b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255371} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e160d2d-13f1-551b-bfa1-abe0c5a5a564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38924e39-789b-5c5e-b0d2-35b2ccc7c0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120752" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x){|}_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100819 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32f8ec3-ac69-5b6a-9937-1093556d24d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Sine or Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01457f0f-a114-52b0-8b99-dee62a99298a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253678} {:answer/id #uuid "39ee2c07-1e95-56ac-b29f-2d31814338ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254179} {:answer/id #uuid "521095f0-b468-5460-9697-086df948f5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254384} {:answer/id #uuid "c0dea304-43bd-5722-b88c-ba6c94f87993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255389} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcc9c58-d321-5486-b9e1-4ca234472367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={330}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01457f0f-a114-52b0-8b99-dee62a99298a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253678} :answer-field/id #uuid "a776a03b-bf9d-569f-a094-dda88cdd9e58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a17c10-d59a-5539-82f1-c464939b55af" :question/math-academy-id "q-120762" :question/problem "In degrees, what is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘},\\,x={315}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a231e4f-d6b3-549f-81d4-30c0a883f9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Secant or Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e43c895-6027-5cd9-9531-b87e49246459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253793} {:answer/id #uuid "1f44fb9c-996a-5031-9466-77d33cafff38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253942} {:answer/id #uuid "78089320-37af-59db-ad4c-cbdf0b14c2e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254732} {:answer/id #uuid "f199d7d2-b656-500b-b205-701c1444dbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255780} {:answer/id #uuid "fcec6c04-971e-5821-b58b-21c7ccfb8873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f199d7d2-b656-500b-b205-701c1444dbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255780} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7caaf7c-63ba-5300-b937-e172c9f59ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbeced00-09aa-5843-8bf0-6c51146e3b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-120771" :question/problem "What is the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/26/269d503189ab1027c3d8028542ef0c6a0e2f146aef0f7916691428866ce1b894.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$  lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa20dbfa-3f6f-5caf-9c10-906f2422f161" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Trigonometric Equation Containing Tangent or Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e27451-1c93-5939-a2aa-3ad5c9f224d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253689} {:answer/id #uuid "30717d72-cb50-585d-851e-60f14a0b4511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254095} {:answer/id #uuid "521cc7bf-20a7-57c9-8c21-bdb3841f5227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254385} {:answer/id #uuid "64b5019a-5776-596d-a112-a4958d9feb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254574} {:answer/id #uuid "ed14bb3c-d140-54cd-ba45-9419f18b4a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64b5019a-5776-596d-a112-a4958d9feb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254574} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9eeed47-fcc7-52c7-8d0d-8d13e6f5b0d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "994feea1-4e21-534e-9a72-c1ddab9354dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-120782" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}.$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nThe tangent function has a period of $\\pi .$  Hence, the general solution for the given equation is\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f6f627-2f5f-5347-9503-ff016a5b6080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253702} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc1c20c-56c7-5e18-9f59-20d43cd28e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253786} {:answer/id #uuid "314c66e0-d6ef-5926-95cd-7d162c322e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254100} {:answer/id #uuid "391480c9-2b7d-5c4c-af59-42877898e593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254165} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7a2ee0-bb9d-53c4-bd98-ed75d405853a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "391480c9-2b7d-5c4c-af59-42877898e593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254165} :answer-field/id #uuid "77493d8d-47a6-584a-b0bf-26e515610f6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "93b7d709-aec4-5e7c-b981-7907596e3508" :question/math-academy-id "q-120807" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}={cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ec5f68-9728-531c-a5ac-13c79f005e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254688} {:answer/id #uuid "94b3e784-be6b-57fa-81c2-67618b3ed429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254976} {:answer/id #uuid "aa29f67a-9529-55c6-826f-4b22638ea11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255174} {:answer/id #uuid "cb767150-a33c-5e2f-9db5-2a5e59fdef7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255477} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f93ec-a0fa-5b8d-8457-2f8b02e7dfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb767150-a33c-5e2f-9db5-2a5e59fdef7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255477} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b1f671-fc3b-5f37-ac21-8a3aecfff2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38275387-16ad-5210-9a86-c85c7908724b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120808" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta +1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify and use $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f638f4-f86a-515f-9f34-79bab5aed9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={28}^{∘},{132}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253720} {:answer/id #uuid "224f6339-4d6f-5e84-b12e-6e6df9f718ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={52}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253977} {:answer/id #uuid "8a43384c-d861-5f3a-b84c-c613ddd7827e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={32}^{∘},{128}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254892} {:answer/id #uuid "a13e94d6-c61b-5c3f-aae3-300caad1d7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{158}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255089} {:answer/id #uuid "d940938c-d2de-57cd-9c70-133a3360d2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d940938c-d2de-57cd-9c70-133a3360d2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255594} :answer-field/id #uuid "7908722b-df99-5603-8d9c-625d78a85041" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "722c6645-d9eb-5787-9276-c39131ce1533" :question/math-academy-id "q-120828" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})=41.810{…}^{∘}≈{41.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${41.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc0c031fe6ccbb40dc77474582b301456f537ee2a649ba2d5b3bca9b1240618f.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{41.81}^{∘} \\\\  & ={138.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092166 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "464e7f61-ecbe-5d92-a768-bdba96ba66de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={41}^{∘},{139}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254263} {:answer/id #uuid "69d72443-b15d-5420-872d-20cf241c18f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={59}^{∘},{121}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254622} {:answer/id #uuid "7e19217d-64a1-5640-bcbd-4834c9ad3d04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={33}^{∘},{147}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254788} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2bbeef-4a86-5df0-80c1-4741f9633531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={49}^{∘},{131}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255037} {:answer/id #uuid "f21259fd-d614-5577-a3a5-f9fb03962578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f21259fd-d614-5577-a3a5-f9fb03962578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255785} :answer-field/id #uuid "9540c28b-d25c-5852-ad5d-ea5925808fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d378f08-b29e-5a1d-8989-f385e5bd482e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120835" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=38.682{…}^{∘}≈{38.68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${38.68}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={38.68}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/12/12b17e3994be5e45af3aca185b22618dd0ec7288fba18a4131387783323247a0.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{38.68}^{∘} \\\\  & ={141.32}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={39}^{∘},{141}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6148f0-c0ef-5ec8-84fa-96f7399895bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254084} {:answer/id #uuid "aa374c04-93de-5b00-a6c3-ee0239b5dbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255176} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec00b1-8b3d-578e-aa9d-b52b625b0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255422} {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} {:answer/id #uuid "f9df6008-3042-5fe7-bfd0-7945f4fd4d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0199b8f-172f-517c-8832-b2230b1dbb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ca042d-c28f-56ba-b846-2e285284fac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "814abbe2-963b-55aa-99fd-84de01c3f66e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120893" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](images/49/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16629348-2577-5c39-8c8c-0f24dbb52c87" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Three Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1beb1460-5a9d-5c09-9b3b-2e0b4ddaf401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253904} {:answer/id #uuid "36da961c-7070-5c7e-a1f8-392f2b069620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254142} {:answer/id #uuid "517e430a-634f-5708-9d27-1aa7f50f0988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254376} {:answer/id #uuid "86b231eb-df60-5b58-86ea-0c5183114af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254863} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d5d610-6f81-55d4-94c4-7726640fd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1beb1460-5a9d-5c09-9b3b-2e0b4ddaf401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253904} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfbd05a9-c8e1-5aa9-a21e-478096d5daed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4031e085-738d-5b9f-898b-4694f1a9c939" :question/math-academy-id "q-120940" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=2\\cdot (2-1)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-1)=4\\cdot (3-1)=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+8=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $4$ | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f607558-5a7f-52df-b344-57f2f84791e9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cosine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1b4c3b-0872-5484-846b-7c31f075326b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d66023e-f256-5b13-916d-42dcaa6c013c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254497} {:answer/id #uuid "791eb4cd-5e49-57d9-b7e6-a52a40c5e041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254743} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} {:answer/id #uuid "be503456-5109-5ce6-90f6-46f497a9e83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff67ebc-3568-54d4-9dbc-25c3ce24692f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254803} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee3993dd-3a89-5d8f-8088-b70e6d9fbfde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1acd4d45-a455-5e34-97cf-5720edb8c513" :question/math-academy-id "q-120981" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{9}{11}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{9}{11})=0.612\\,554…≈0.612\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.612\\,6$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.612\\,6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.612\\,6.$\n\n![](images/23/23712114dae7013de5786e51b99cbd0857c41ed6443e0cb6a9f54aa4ba00dcbb.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.612\\,6 \\\\  & ≈5.670\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.612\\,6\\cdot 5.670\\,6≈3.5\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Final Position of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4855a8b7-7a6b-58fd-bfcb-512c8d36c92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254289} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddb2256-cdec-5fd5-b64a-ffbc48c96995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340} {:answer/id #uuid "b63f2399-b1ef-59c8-be8b-f72934d61b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255283} {:answer/id #uuid "ef612402-f1a0-55cb-8215-9500ae958c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255762} {:answer/id #uuid "faccb7a5-43af-5b0a-8c12-37d5995f9f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ddb2256-cdec-5fd5-b64a-ffbc48c96995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254340} :answer-field/id #uuid "73fd2e63-bc3b-516e-aa50-4cef260983d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ad853df-f7ff-5b1c-95e8-898b94e2247f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120998" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2t+1}.$ At time $t=0.5$ the particle is located at the position $x={e}^{2}.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=0.5$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0.5,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(0.5)+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & ={e}^{2}+{\\int }_{0.5}^{2}{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+{[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+1}]}_{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(2)+1}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(0.5)+1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=\\frac{{e}^{2}+{e}^{5}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97786302-e865-5581-b08e-8da7c6b7a01b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4658f5ed-bea8-55d3-a5c8-e35701a6a2f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254264} {:answer/id #uuid "98577715-7654-563c-bb0e-d13248802bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255018} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b96a5b-8ec0-5a2c-b97f-ec596610936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255310} {:answer/id #uuid "d7eeb10e-7c5a-5705-a0a6-9546f983b82d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255585} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cee127-4c04-5f38-b790-e5f2e404fedf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b96a5b-8ec0-5a2c-b97f-ec596610936d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255310} :answer-field/id #uuid "82979c27-ebd4-5fae-902f-a919bb5b2e12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6a9809a-4ab2-58a8-8350-cebc2231a946" :question/math-academy-id "q-121066" :question/problem "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +\\frac{n\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{18}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=-\\frac{1}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092167 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c7ab50-5a8b-5bcd-8483-cec8bf6bd3ce" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a65183c-2bf9-5a8f-ab7a-90a1855419e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253750} {:answer/id #uuid "13ce42f3-cac8-5bb4-9066-effdabcf2334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={63}^{∘},{243}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253840} {:answer/id #uuid "38b6122c-c8d7-5ed6-b92c-eefe16988d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={76}^{∘},{256}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254163} {:answer/id #uuid "748b63c3-6f43-569d-9e36-9b9a22a9eb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={70}^{∘},{250}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254700} {:answer/id #uuid "96a15fbc-f4a7-5fd5-b3c5-d8d3244e644a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={88}^{∘},{268}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a65183c-2bf9-5a8f-ab7a-90a1855419e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253750} :answer-field/id #uuid "409bc980-f28a-5a07-b298-2291184f8da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1252830-b58d-56d0-beaa-bb7162765f44" :question/math-academy-id "q-121127" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=8$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ rounded to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(8)=82.874\\,983{…}^{∘}≈{82.87}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${82.87}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={82.87}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{82.87}^{∘}={262.87}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx={83}^{∘},{263}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0737c6dd-2968-5cc9-a8f8-c0079a8268a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253724} {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} {:answer/id #uuid "75bbd111-a6df-57f9-ac2f-45af1364cecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254712} {:answer/id #uuid "8398ba0c-a692-519d-9e74-4243e928998b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254841} {:answer/id #uuid "acc2456f-4e14-5655-aa35-0e7518c63750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7037b1e7-eb89-5813-ae66-13b1ef312f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/math-academy-id "q-121221" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129218 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9540b1c6-1f60-5074-bc72-614497b1d511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126021} {:answer/id #uuid "225ef1cc-e171-581b-9168-b62af522eaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}