frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot \\cancel{10!}}{\\cancel{10!}\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083312 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111b2cfd-82b0-5772-b24e-fd1bd23e4b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253819} {:answer/id #uuid "21171d16-53d1-5b90-a331-d3226cde70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253963} {:answer/id #uuid "46c7e24a-c1fd-5962-ac5d-4839e3b8c67a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254268} {:answer/id #uuid "6832b881-4d09-5782-a46a-848fa67f6fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254613} {:answer/id #uuid "78be4145-2565-5481-9bb8-5816c8527f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21171d16-53d1-5b90-a331-d3226cde70e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253963} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6b1a42-ec84-598a-a9a5-a2d349f20b27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9dcfba9-1011-5da0-85ce-637573b456f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122498" :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\frac{7}{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\frac{7}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{1!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ec50a16-bb6b-5f05-b165-80436402a0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a47123d-f4ec-5210-b67f-61a4869b33fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254046} {:answer/id #uuid "3b29dafa-efbf-5a37-8d32-4a83de611c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254185} {:answer/id #uuid "82e767a8-42f1-58ae-87ff-b6da314b6877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254834} {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c081a7f4-7f7e-5025-bc7b-e08baf4bd4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255383} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6ca1418-3eca-579f-bc5d-83202ced8211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d62b2335-132d-58f4-9c9e-53b886749092" :question/math-academy-id "q-122550" :question/problem "In a group of six dancers, if every possible pair dances together, what is the total number of dances?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ people, so $n=6.$ We want to select $2$ of those people for each dance, so we have $r=2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of dances is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{6}{C}_{2} & =\\frac{6!}{(6-2)!\\,2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{4!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d412dbf-4d63-56a8-97f9-9b35e1f750a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,807" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253916} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b6064-5a8e-5103-a78f-a2c0f2e9b9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254054} {:answer/id #uuid "9aef58db-6ad9-5889-81a4-34935e92803f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255035} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf8596c-104e-5f0a-9f7f-a01c66e5d834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255183} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b62d0a-3b46-5af4-9af2-0aff7507291f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf8596c-104e-5f0a-9f7f-a01c66e5d834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255183} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1052dc-4eee-5989-a0a2-a3cf9141dddf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4102b5a-9ebe-5326-97ea-80b1e81d8483" :question/math-academy-id "q-122551" :question/problem "Mike wants to work out five days a week. In how many ways can Mike choose five days to work out?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ days in a week, so $n=7.$ We want to select $5$ of those days for Mike to workout, so we have $r=5.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\,5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{2!5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2!\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28decb17-2f60-5f59-a7f6-7cd775afb238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254037} {:answer/id #uuid "383c9e5e-11b5-576b-8182-b0c4670f77ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\,480" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254156} {:answer/id #uuid "4821aeac-b15c-52cb-80e2-ce26701d85ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254284} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8875a4-0a40-525e-86e4-1aa56ad397a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,728" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254650} {:answer/id #uuid "9e619d41-571d-589f-8e3b-716a7b602217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4821aeac-b15c-52cb-80e2-ce26701d85ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254284} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6f2b63-feb8-5d1c-b0ef-b85df01026cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0df0d84e-0701-5f25-a0a7-891dc8357fe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122560" :question/problem "Claire has a bag of candies with $12$ assorted flavors. In how many ways can Claire pick $3$ flavors of candies?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ flavors, so $n=12.$ We want to select $3$ of those flavors, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{12}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{12!}{(12-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12!}{9!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10\\cdot \\cancel{9!}}{\\cancel{9!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 11\\cdot 10}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,320}{6} \\\\  & =220.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2483de-bb9b-57f7-a79f-34885e11205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253803} {:answer/id #uuid "c65aceae-e84b-5651-9ab5-ee308d3662ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb258778-9d8d-556c-8e5b-b5614a8787a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255476} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0c7cf4-bb80-57c1-a9c1-3db0dee8005d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d4061c-5bca-5874-a889-0e5b0bcb0553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "405e30d2-1994-59ac-94e7-5e7998f82913" :question/math-academy-id "q-122698" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51f9b53-0ce5-518f-8173-abe4d87f2851" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Image of an Object Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c65048-1bd8-5574-a9e0-ec600f77776e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254007} {:answer/id #uuid "2943610c-b458-56d4-bb3f-f27b602d8f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254039} {:answer/id #uuid "7222c014-590a-580d-bcd2-5f89ee56ea92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254681} {:answer/id #uuid "7702c729-8b14-568b-ab41-781a2678ec26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254723} {:answer/id #uuid "a9281d40-c16f-5476-80ec-f24aab8ca235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25c65048-1bd8-5574-a9e0-ec600f77776e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254007} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fdbd67e-cb8a-553c-8fd4-1077676fab3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb707a49-2312-560f-8806-ec259a1b8ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-12283" :question/problem "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/3c/3cbf4ce4422e043cedb73e9b10bba11e9cb1e64352ff182eff8aaa0046c4e8f3.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=4\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,10\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a82d5f6-66f3-5522-a100-e2f8dae13966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387320} {:answer/id #uuid "629adac7-7e11-5b16-8d75-97adcdbde8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387321} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbc0dc2-d7b9-50b2-a002-118c594c07e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387322} {:answer/id #uuid "08ad482e-16a9-5965-ad6e-397324dce8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387323} {:answer/id #uuid "6be35ef2-6065-55cf-b015-361f21dd7ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a82d5f6-66f3-5522-a100-e2f8dae13966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387320} :answer-field/id #uuid "299f6ec7-a02a-58c0-aab8-a90cf14d427a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33942b97-95c2-5a05-bee1-52e88505e10f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12284" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y=3 \\\\ 2x-y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "588836e4-5eb6-541f-8c8b-b6376ce409bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254447} {:answer/id #uuid "a67f757f-f076-5974-98f9-9c295d1adbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255137} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe875b-a80a-59e4-bcdb-ab5d745e6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} {:answer/id #uuid "d1028432-c454-5405-bd1d-355788a24a19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255534} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b6eea0-760d-5988-ad2c-b73df215fded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2fe875b-a80a-59e4-bcdb-ab5d745e6341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255409} :answer-field/id #uuid "075ced90-6313-50ba-a2ba-09025bda52b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9232c011-690c-5d8a-aa34-6b7d7e62803f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122888" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-10,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+15.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t))}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f344a5d-f20b-5d29-bc4b-455872b79c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254087} {:answer/id #uuid "9e617510-b480-5d70-b155-ca6d7883d5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255062} {:answer/id #uuid "b77a05d6-9403-5a91-8f6f-1d9bf6e028b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255297} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa266e3-b7f3-5389-b0e2-d0701e510855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255519} {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523} :answer-field/id #uuid "38fc7afa-faeb-58be-bde2-8b46b42e1581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f7632b-4b69-541a-a152-af5eeab4cd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122898" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068778 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17cd4781-75f9-5d54-92c9-5a81fe089f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253876} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ceae-51b0-5202-9628-0a5e4a9020a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254212} {:answer/id #uuid "6caeeb15-4378-5ace-8068-b8fd6f439c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254636} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c4ed0f-fb38-5f89-af25-35fa5ce2b7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255289} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2d00ca-afde-5e4c-bf42-d140dddc73ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ceae-51b0-5202-9628-0a5e4a9020a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254212} :answer-field/id #uuid "49798dcc-1822-5938-bc33-2012ca7f0a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d9e720b-9be0-5b91-a998-bd0d620bd59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-123003" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-10,\\,y=4{t}^{6}+5.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12{t}^{4},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=12{t}^{4},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-10)=2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(12{t}^{4})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{48{t}^{3}}{2t} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=24\\cdot {1}^{2}=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221b2a78-3ed9-5fcc-a904-b72ed2d8cda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400088} {:answer/id #uuid "2037ceca-da9b-5e6c-99af-f9e9635c71f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+6{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400089} {:answer/id #uuid "f6282b39-25dd-527c-8c08-c5ccbd4d5888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+12{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400090} {:answer/id #uuid "3954ccd4-113c-522e-8865-af8dedf5924f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400091} {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f536300-dcae-55c0-9423-568faa002b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400092} :answer-field/id #uuid "065d677c-9998-56e4-8b57-ce841d04bf36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30eadd5a-469c-54ec-b683-aec3a996fde4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123030" :question/problem "![](images/88/8868658c9bc7195f435a0109fce26b8a4fa39324c0a9a39e3fb644c5f02cdc9b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ccd52-63db-5d28-b78b-2df7caf52ad0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b97203e9-8d87-5cac-b645-904ef91cae7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387355} {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387356} {:answer/id #uuid "5941fefb-b666-5264-9ff1-ea630cf1bd88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,\\,\\frac{1}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387357} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8b17a6-4cbf-54f9-99c3-f7a62dd1829d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,\\,\\frac{4}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387358} {:answer/id #uuid "53d7fc04-e503-5637-99f3-3e1a31b96eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f63b47e-4384-5932-bde6-b16ca9aebd87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387356} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7722d9c-8a8e-51c2-9846-c98ae9635908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12003e8d-c48c-5642-87cc-c7c437d424fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-123203" :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)⟩,$ calculate $r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our function, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}),\\,{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04325cf9-d54a-5e1e-83f1-34996b512ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814} {:answer/id #uuid "4613da6a-11c4-57ad-b9bc-ce9a8d92fd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257396} {:answer/id #uuid "83d8d0fa-6f25-5545-acfd-ab53e9d8056d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257947} {:answer/id #uuid "a53a4c0c-1058-5236-aa4f-579fc051583d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258222} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a95397-8087-572e-9dd6-fff5d617108b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04325cf9-d54a-5e1e-83f1-34996b512ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256814} :answer-field/id #uuid "f598b175-ab79-5c53-b43d-323efda15bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da384ddd-c430-57ef-b97f-337efd7bbd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-123703" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1$ for $n>1?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=-1+{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{{log}_{2}⁡n}-1)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e00ff7-a91e-5b56-9f3a-24e7318cf004" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence Sequences Containing Power and Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e5f1225-603f-5ec0-b0df-80e9e3432495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257904} {:answer/id #uuid "ace9a51b-9513-5ddc-8a3b-6ba6a0c6474e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The sequence diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258291} {:answer/id #uuid "acf5a79b-214a-5003-abd2-c44fa7bb32f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258293} {:answer/id #uuid "c86d1e95-b8b3-56ae-afb5-bd21da9e243d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258530} {:answer/id #uuid "ff24ade0-2e8c-5362-9179-50f3bb65d1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86d1e95-b8b3-56ae-afb5-bd21da9e243d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258530} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace598bf-e616-5960-82bd-2a0c51e47391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c802f236-b44c-53ec-8248-9ae94870d716" :question/math-academy-id "q-123707" :question/problem "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}$ for $n\\ge 1$ converge or diverge? If the sequence converges, what is its limit?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(100n)}{n\\sqrt{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges,  and its limit is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51d50237-f6cf-5c2d-b8d1-335378205a28" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of Sequences Containing Exponential Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c474915-3112-5a65-ba46-8702d62ab30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396636} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd4e9ae-e251-5b16-b1c3-5b9bc45cef41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396637} {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396638} {:answer/id #uuid "0b06ae43-fe31-53fd-8b10-6540097b006e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396639} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a0eaea-fc7b-55da-b356-f336150395ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{n}$ and ${c}_{n}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bce095d-cbe6-5499-a685-c442389e61af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396638} :answer-field/id #uuid "520b0925-7644-5da3-a57b-98c58f41393e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b9bf7b26-4c35-5b3c-bd82-2290add25e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-123715" :question/problem "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n\\ge 1,$ converge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087736 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "befcfe4a-abd3-5984-a1a3-1a1bf2b0cd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400123} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee13255-e4e9-52e6-b06e-d0aeddb3bb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400124} {:answer/id #uuid "c3236814-4c23-5fc9-a82d-b9432370c830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-3\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400125} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd316cc-7219-5b56-b38c-fa0c32ea3d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400126} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb2b3dc-0e10-5966-a703-a0088b75ec08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400127} :answer-field/id #uuid "806b0a66-23a8-530b-9fd2-35a679c8dd3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42535d46-cb00-5afc-82ab-e62c0ea06521" :question/math-academy-id "q-123773" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\ge -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -1 \\\\ -1 & \\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 1-1 & \\le 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+1 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le 1+2 \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3809196-0e72-568a-acb4-8f1806b011f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1cec5ea2da4a0b5f141bb24b4addc797defbc48e76cdfdbfab3a303e348b8cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387439} {:answer/id #uuid "d44a46da-960a-5000-b573-28968c62249a" :answer/type {:db/