er/id #uuid "9ce385bc-2516-5301-a4cb-0ce65a749f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258162} {:answer/id #uuid "b22bab3f-9316-5310-8fb4-b670b1d10da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258333} {:answer/id #uuid "f1239ea4-4271-56cc-bc8e-fe1a2fd69654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b22bab3f-9316-5310-8fb4-b670b1d10da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258333} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b4e062-0d53-5765-889c-2a0da239ec0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cbd5c1c-b243-5354-99fa-1a41e7c9ebe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-124475" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0ad5279a7ec9ac20a2c4e1449f93ae67854caf021aba8435165b80069f23afbd.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠B={60}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠B,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{AC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+2) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+2\\sqrt{3} & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2=4\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+2=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90a5c75b-e93d-5f09-a126-3d0cb5ede2d5" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Factorial in an Equivalent Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fae7a58-5f8a-59f7-a26b-e37ea84449ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258044} {:answer/id #uuid "ac295ae4-0956-533a-95bb-43fe80dfb821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258282} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c69797-b7c1-5c9b-8c12-6254b4fc206c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258358} {:answer/id #uuid "c63b6375-a6af-5c2b-bb3b-f9ac6aca359c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+1)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258515} {:answer/id #uuid "efaa4806-083a-546d-8433-3487b50984f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac295ae4-0956-533a-95bb-43fe80dfb821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258282} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03fcfaa-96c1-5bd9-a600-2d70d1ca8a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12e390ab-b9d1-50cd-bae8-a1a0dd93fc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-124498" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $(n+3)!$ for any $n\\ge -1?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the definition of factorial, we have\n\n$$\n(n+3)!=(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n+3)! & =(n+3)\\cdot \\overset{(n+2)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(n+3)\\cdot (n+2)!\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037a7c54-c302-5789-b25e-bd233adb82e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256811} {:answer/id #uuid "0bf43ace-4823-5eaa-9ad1-0be3fc1525b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898} {:answer/id #uuid "45b120b9-4ece-5a16-a391-5bfbb0ea9f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257392} {:answer/id #uuid "ad55cbe1-d31f-5103-bded-f0845d5ae71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258296} {:answer/id #uuid "c5881a22-3400-5139-bc65-47b813f402ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf43ace-4823-5eaa-9ad1-0be3fc1525b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256898} :answer-field/id #uuid "9474a253-0604-56e8-84bf-d3c9bd812162" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7d8485d-2f78-5d59-9916-1d1cd2c548de" :question/math-academy-id "q-124608" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11b11b-3274-5c34-b09c-f5ec78de4e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257177} {:answer/id #uuid "4dcebdf8-f732-50f7-b15a-29792fc1f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257450} {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} {:answer/id #uuid "5d59303b-e2ee-5904-a3d2-9f17c7fcfaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257597} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb144b3-687e-5d43-94a0-00b76d2854a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52f6bed6-51b4-5841-9d86-c1222468a4fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257491} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd602cdd-cbaf-5de8-8895-9b474285c26d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66205715-ba82-541e-8ac0-31ae0009bc90" :question/math-academy-id "q-124611" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ae39b9-8d77-5506-95cb-36cfda6b93ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258226} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ca8282-18dd-559f-b3d0-161a4153c226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258329} {:answer/id #uuid "f50445f6-c5b6-5037-8d82-ae09d76d2629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258921} {:answer/id #uuid "f57c9ed2-c68c-53ab-85fa-4cb0d40fff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} :answer-field/id #uuid "62707eaf-bd66-53e1-b24c-8b731568b056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "186cddc1-d1ed-556b-9213-9d3efc27c116" :question/math-academy-id "q-124743" :question/problem "![](images/02/02cb693b74f0f315389f87ceed1674da11f661b3363e4df0890bdf82d61cc60c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a4/a479e8e7906a3a0dc43e925c5bccd6534b4ab2e02a289403b2d0c92d0b0eb1d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148d9fa6-e709-5ed3-8dee-a9794d49d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256967} {:answer/id #uuid "57004998-41ca-5e4e-90f5-2c24561a08c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee702e6-7a9d-5c39-9f0f-93b8b0fe4698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} {:answer/id #uuid "d244da59-6698-5edd-a3a0-8e822fc76895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615} {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654efa3-cd29-5e20-bbaf-04bd03e93e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba2d6f5-ab4c-55d2-aafd-8aaa2c0dbeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "374059a8-6fd5-53d5-82e6-c0ca083f7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257267} {:answer/id #uuid "605bf4ad-ad76-590d-bd34-06fd61323e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257629} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca6d0e9-6ce5-54df-867d-65c15a711c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258017} {:answer/id #uuid "9e56d472-5870-5827-b0a4-e0552ef27936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258174} {:answer/id #uuid "a6765343-206b-5392-8a7c-8689b6c899f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6765343-206b-5392-8a7c-8689b6c899f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258234} :answer-field/id #uuid "affc45c9-4666-5a81-994f-df2750e5a840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0991dc0-bba8-5452-beb4-9ef876fd9e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-124816" :question/problem "![](images/36/3687e362fea73defca43332ee70f232282ad1eded9dc14cbbc045de4324067f0.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $-\\frac{6}{7}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{6}{7}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{6}{7})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{36}{49}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{36}{49} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{13}{49} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $x$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100891 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3b5e36-46db-5d34-92e2-eefb7e0e95c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3064ab7-54be-5462-ae53-ab47118bf7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387611} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7e2f0c-1e2b-5ea8-be50-daf51e2e9626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387612} {:answer/id #uuid "d7168e0d-4c71-55fb-b5dc-472312e878ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+315{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387613} {:answer/id #uuid "fa744589-a280-5c0b-adc9-a65dd97443bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+120{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387614} {:answer/id #uuid "a8bf4816-a669-55ac-a5bd-36b3025ebd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+10{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3064ab7-54be-5462-ae53-ab47118bf7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387611} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b383894-df41-50ee-a309-d940d6accc1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e01d5c53-05d5-5886-b4aa-a254711880e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-12490" :question/problem "${(x+7y)}^{10}=$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ $n=10.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x+7y)}^{10}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10} & =(\\frac{10}{0}){(x)}^{10}{(7y)}^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){(x)}^{10-1}{(7y)}^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){(x)}^{10-2}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1{(x)}^{10}+10{(x)}^{9}{(7y)}^{1}+45{(x)}^{8}{(7y)}^{2}+… \\\\  & =1({x}^{10})+10({x}^{9})(7y)+45({x}^{8})(49{y}^{2})+… \\\\  & ={x}^{10}+70{x}^{9}y+2\\,205{x}^{8}{y}^{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a00fa7-a425-5b28-941c-bc8027676ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Convergence of a Geometric Series With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e46ab2a-0d85-55db-8491-fba51724229e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257192} {:answer/id #uuid "aef3ae68-bae1-5af0-a668-e2364f52124c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258306} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf28ae8-5f15-5ec7-86b9-394cae91a3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Inconclusive because $|r|=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258430} {:answer/id #uuid "cefdd06c-68a0-575a-bf3f-32bd5c90e1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258580} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7c966c-5bce-5757-bbb7-73e91bf9ca04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff7c966c-5bce-5757-bbb7-73e91bf9ca04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The series is convergent because $|r|<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259027} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48b7d2b-0745-503d-90a0-7066afe96786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a13d78b9-6e79-57bd-8107-15b9e5813662" :question/math-academy-id "q-124900" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series  ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(-0.3)}^{n}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=4.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(-0.3)}^{5}}{{(-0.3)}^{4}} \\\\  & =-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62736299-f6d6-503f-a5d1-e1091e8b0429" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3c60d79-86b9-50cc-9f64-560ea2cecec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400151} {:answer/id #uuid "04fc169e-d7b2-5fb6-aaaa-03529f82ef7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400152} {:answer/id #uuid "c64b1ae1-73f4-50e4-83d1-af39cac88db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400153} {:answer/id #uuid "c0007124-207e-55b6-82bb-cf42de7dce86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400154} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3720ee-33a3-5bc3-abd0-5c477d7133fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d3720ee-33a3-5bc3-abd0-5c477d7133fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400155} :answer-field/id #uuid "7478616a-dd06-5aea-b75a-b73c688ab5fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55c1789a-3f1b-5ebc-a34c-eaab270f436c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124912" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $-1+1-1+1-⋯$\nII. $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$\nIII. $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $-1+1-1+1-⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $\\frac{4}{3}-1+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{9}{16}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(\\frac{4}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $0.1+0.11+0.121+0.133\\,1+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{0.11}{0.1} \\\\  & =1.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent  because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "908ce57e-7ca8-5e5f-bdcb-0d2ef79f10ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Range of Values of a Parameter for Which a Geometric Series Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62955adc-8c6b-5d3b-b579-be842cd89d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257651} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf589c8-9b8a-5528-adc6-3eebf8416a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258009} {:answer/id #uuid "93e2b0db-8dfd-5411-8b29-1363f0bda390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258087} {:answer/id #uuid "bcffd714-06e5-5020-b0b7-7b2921d626d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258431} {:answer/id #uuid "f52dd78d-755d-5967-be24-ba80b171f1bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcffd714-06e5-5020-b0b7-7b2921d626d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258431} :answer-field/id #uuid "85782518-1371-5479-9454-ede5672eec41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a499578c-6384-52f3-8461-3e78463b6725" :question/math-academy-id "q-124924" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn-1}}{{100}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{10}^{kn}}{{100}^{n}\\cdot 10} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{10}^{k}}{100} & <1 \\\\ {10}^{k} & <100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{10}^{k}}{100}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${10}^{k}=100$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${10}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${10}^{k}<100$ must be $k<2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14557b18-0563-5b53-9f86-fa3ca7f47384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256963} {:answer/id #uuid "655311f9-e5d1-5870-9895-37bfc0e5bf06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257671} {:answer/id #uuid "764ac68c-5a27-54cd-b524-a31f92868b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257820} {:answer/id #uuid "82af07e0-822b-5ca8-9bee-31fb9e99ddde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257940} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb1be87-9bec-5261-a137-45f33fdea218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "764ac68c-5a27-54cd-b524-a31f92868b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257820} :answer-field/id #uuid "6318d8bf-6cd1-5740-986d-80321788e4a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5732cef-fd51-5b54-b80c-b4a2a52357b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124958" :question/problem "If $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sin⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e887638bb8b3b135c6a8e6eecf564fe8310a43f2ce26f8b565ec6966bafb89c.png)\n\nWe're given that $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the second quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129081 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283723ea-a88e-58ae-b5e7-50b3adc30b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257138} {:answer/id #uuid "554dab01-2781-5b3b-904c-6f7365f441e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257512} {:answer/id #uuid "a9807580-b8ed-5e0b-9685-81cacc71955c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258262} {:answer/id #uuid "cba11a29-a963-50dc-95d2-18ae2dc7ff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258547} {:answer/id #uuid "f47fdff0-7ad2-5bd4-8cd6-52ab13350e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cba11a29-a963-50dc-95d2-18ae2dc7ff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258547} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e570dc4-28db-5cee-8f25-148cbb584b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f344f8f2-1c82-536a-b6c4-df0e2bd726d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-124959" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ and ${90}^{∘}<\\theta <{180}^{∘}$ then what is the value of $cos⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f6/f6c2b0f2e6b8c9b10de0341a73d8c7d1d88ba64bd046e82fee8db37828ca2555.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0439a2aa-fc95-5a6f-8ba6-a08e185beaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256815} {:answer/id #uuid "76cdbd0e-31fa-5d43-9da9-087458569c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257830} {:answer/id #uuid "7a976b23-fe40-5cb5-af5e-04ee0fd15dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257866} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4cbcec-1f82-59d7-95a8-2396d64d3e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{36}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258449} {:answer/id #uuid "cb446671-4285-5ffd-806b-31e615004133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76cdbd0e-31fa-5d43-9da9-087458569c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{10}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257830} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ede77b-ed26-5e38-bf4c-454ee0d7c02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "395d59bc-74f3-5209-b445-2d063af44516" :question/math-academy-id "q-12496" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{3}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the ${x}^{3}$ that's outside the parentheses isn't quite the derivative of $({x}^{4}+11).$ But the derivative is $4{x}^{3},$ so we're only out by a constant factor. \n\nSo we make the substitution\n\n$$\nu={x}^{4}+11.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =4{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u={x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can now write our integral in terms of $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{({x}^{4}+11)}^{9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{4}+11)^{9}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{9}\\,\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{9}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{u}^{10}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}({x}^{4}+11)^{10}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1e516a-fd02-539c-a55b-1d53b977ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400158} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd19dff-a905-5735-9496-24d7b4eee41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400159} {:answer/id #uuid "7175eeec-eda1-5a92-99be-f76806feffc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400160} {:answer/id #uuid "14302d62-f56c-5807-8418-d9e29a49a8ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400161} {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3a9c05-b642-53bc-aee7-47f4b93b0bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400162} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec6cfae-f16c-5f01-8f87-2f00e0b258ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bb1887b-828c-58c3-9b10-3c0e7dc6d40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124965" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd41c969aee2e7c04c9034106978d8522d6a715733098bfde87abbdb861da7a8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4},$ what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/49/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4})}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064210 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2feb2115-bd75-5c09-87cc-f10cf46c182f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals of Algebraic Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1207805e-dbad-515d-bc59-d46dd549c7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{45}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{27}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256947} {:answer/id #uuid "23b06bd9-e43a-5ce9-a4e7-de33b7c00919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257082} {:answer/id #uuid "3608d43b-fbe5-5b9b-bd38-918370a2164f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{45}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{9}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+С" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257257} {:answer/id #uuid "7942ecf7-c33c-59a7-b547-e0aad2228d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+С" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257854} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c1b674-3f07-5054-8b62-29b4dede0eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{27}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1207805e-dbad-515d-bc59-d46dd549c7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{45}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{27}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256947} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f415475-9d2f-5036-8871-5dd5bdc3295b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf091dac-5b0f-53e6-bd72-c4b61f020711" :question/math-academy-id "q-12497" :question/problem "$\\int x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=3x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=\\frac{u-1}{3}.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{u-1}{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int ({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{1}{5/2}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{3/2}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{27}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{27}\\sqrt{(3x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064205 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae5760b-9277-596c-8546-dca4fd985bd7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Algebraic Function With Fractional Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c00a40ba-01e3-5ee1-8e78-5ca100de2c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395730} {:answer/id #uuid "b95804dd-01ed-5465-b1ec-454869fcea4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395731} {:answer/id #uuid "957bc594-a52d-54c5-8fe2-348b94cdf190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{5x+1}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395732} {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} {:answer/id #uuid "2458c64b-d709-5fd5-a6bf-7d12e7bb36e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40a36a09-4403-51a9-8c9b-14cdbfe2aa41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bf9bf65-124a-5233-aedc-81c57617266f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "457b9ae0-36a8-577c-85ad-8057dc66fc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12498" :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=5x+1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5x+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{u}^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(5x+1)^{3/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{2}{15}\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(5x+1)^{3/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5x+1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(5x+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(5x+1)^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5x+1}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbeb0313-5651-561b-a4b7-42ac4f88f1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400165} {:answer/id #uuid "1a165698-e8f8-506c-9756-3955aee8c79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400166} {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3e631f-f822-5565-8077-5a496dd0604b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{15}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400168} {:answer/id #uuid "0944c75f-3871-5029-b2ac-06fe51638caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-8\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a56d6b-1f3c-53ed-9db5-88987f60fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta =-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400167} :answer-field/id #uuid "77272688-3fb6-5eaf-8b2d-59ba189820e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c006e542-29a2-5102-8308-98f4920ebd17" :question/math-academy-id "q-125004" :question/problem "If $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/5c/5c98414bbe0e1e373864e9ec1d6b8667bb0032c8824f20bed1e6f9b9079c4d02.png)\n\nWe're given that $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{7}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{49}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{15}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8})}=-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{15}}=-\\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453def53-174d-5896-8e4f-7faa7466d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{({x}^{2}+5)}^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257385} {:answer/id #uuid "98eb7e12-8b7e-5878-badc-a9f99309abb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258129} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b2fef7-0445-5ee0-9c9b-2663d3fb7feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258199} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcefc88-d3ad-5c21-9ac6-75e0800e4041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{8}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258438} {:answer/id #uuid "dfeda5a9-a962-51e6-b8b1-1e93608cc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfeda5a9-a962-51e6-b8b1-1e93608cc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18{({x}^{2}+5)}^{9}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258733} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7f8515-44cd-5765-b9d4-3675220879f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d839b9a-ccae-57dc-9109-2e848ce0d27f" :question/math-academy-id "q-12502" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+5.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand we can solve the resulting integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+5)}^{10}}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{10}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{10}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{9{u}^{9}})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18{u}^{9}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18({x}^{2}+5)^{9}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1827d656-4f38-58c3-9c5b-1c67546538b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256991} {:answer/id #uuid "32bfcc38-31e9-5d2f-badb-ee4c824315ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257227} {:answer/id #uuid "456aed63-63fa-53ba-b339-360b7c281de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257388} {:answer/id #uuid "672887e0-6a58-55e0-8acd-3737b9cde108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257696} {:answer/id #uuid "88fcaba1-1bbc-5f64-9901-f6af9715c201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32bfcc38-31e9-5d2f-badb-ee4c824315ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257227} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d934f0-2090-5c9f-adcc-cfc7fc664974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73c775bc-183c-5f70-94e3-d84df278daa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125248" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec58e17c04427988e634bebdece555ad782be504bd0734dbd7ffd5b1b95bc760.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,3).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=3.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-5)}{3-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-3)}{3+5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{8}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95363d69-aef8-5be0-bed6-2e48e831157e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400172} {:answer/id #uuid "c811cfee-b520-5024-a08a-7253a95a06d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400173} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4caf99-22b3-54c0-8115-85030a8148f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400174} {:answer/id #uuid "7f361f8f-c150-58fe-9b01-71b7b26b992c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400175} {:answer/id #uuid "f957daf2-0051-5201-9483-2ef71d3af41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb4caf99-22b3-54c0-8115-85030a8148f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400174} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ce3352c-5866-54ec-8397-cf9e37019fd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0d26c1fd-9972-5992-b3ee-1cc9fed13d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-125262" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4791998a-2e12-50fa-8127-8fe3482a030d" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Maclaurin Series of a Function in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62ef9b1a-c5b1-592f-905c-1148c6bb7a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257655} {:answer/id #uuid "7dac31d0-cc33-5ea3-9be3-753aa8ecdb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257893} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec3ac33-c68c-5c8e-aad0-966891e72d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258040} {:answer/id #uuid "9653f25b-fe72-55c8-a0b5-e5865057edff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258107} {:answer/id #uuid "e66ee4c6-8871-50fa-a70a-7afd0f7be6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62ef9b1a-c5b1-592f-905c-1148c6bb7a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257655} :answer-field/id #uuid "04939a54-4fe5-5b91-9a96-83f4df820d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f817f43-3880-5280-8951-789b899f0b7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125340" :question/problem "Express the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}=x+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}+⋯,\\,x\\in (-1,1)\n$$\n\nusing sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "The first four terms of the series are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =x \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can express these terms using a common format, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{{x}^{2\\cdot 0+1}}{2\\cdot 0+1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2\\cdot 1+1}}{2\\cdot 1+1} \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2\\cdot 2+1}}{2\\cdot 2+1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}^{2\\cdot 3+1}}{2\\cdot 3+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe deduce that the general formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{2n+1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{1-x}}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{2n+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096728 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6482f0f2-1c1d-5fea-a1fb-3579ff2c32b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\ne P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396013} {:answer/id #uuid "229bdc3a-27fc-56da-aa6a-896f13040c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396014} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb8d3e3-cdce-512d-ad4f-36dcf03d5780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\cdot P(B)<P(A\\cup B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396015} {:answer/id #uuid "909efe4d-2b15-56ed-93cf-072fb3e590e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396016} {:answer/id #uuid "4c73addd-4987-575f-af08-f65362be8bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A)\\ne P(B)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "909efe4d-2b15-56ed-93cf-072fb3e590e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events, since $P(A\\cap B)\\ne 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396016} :answer-field/id #uuid "50eb031e-ead1-553b-9ace-42643ea8d178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5829032-59a0-5111-b1d8-47c7a5a05911" :question/math-academy-id "q-12549" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=0.25$, $P(B)=0.75$, $P(A\\cup B)=0.8$, determine whether  $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.8 & =0.25+0.75-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.25+0.75-0.8 \\\\  & =0.2\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c9d1dd-a829-5c18-bb18-ca56df8ad1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257135} {:answer/id #uuid "3beb4623-daae-5393-8669-4daa629096ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257318} {:answer/id #uuid "61cf46c7-6594-5fd1-bf08-ff7ed2ce3b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{52}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257643} {:answer/id #uuid "93e1e01b-bdbc-5286-b832-72582f1169aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258086} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b4d7bc-c79c-5165-bbd7-fe88098486b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{51}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27c9d1dd-a829-5c18-bb18-ca56df8ad1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257135} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d72d14-22ba-5156-a601-f88934bbfb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d422248-baa9-5e0e-a9ed-604bdfcad48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-12554" :question/problem "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} {:answer/id #uuid "4ceb3c00-a293-5837-a92d-52eeb50f0ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400187} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca2de-1a70-5b67-a36e-991a07dbc939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400188} {:answer/id #uuid "9f38d46b-1ede-55d8-a14d-a43663a75ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400189} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab42314-64ed-5e2f-b8e6-3a69d859b6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3912714-af31-5641-8bff-9b46c1aa3234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400186} :answer-field/id #uuid "441fdc82-6754-52e3-ae25-4987eecf3c9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b30b956-8f97-5e5a-bc88-28b6d6148226" :question/math-academy-id "q-125549" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d783281-8364-5597-a804-a4ea0d76bec8" :knowl